{"id":3778,"date":"2021-09-24T13:18:35","date_gmt":"2021-09-24T20:18:35","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=3778"},"modified":"2023-08-21T08:35:39","modified_gmt":"2023-08-21T15:35:39","slug":"2-7-user-input-controller","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/2-7-user-input-controller\/","title":{"rendered":"2.7: User Input Controller"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221480394e0\" name=\"6a221480394e0\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Goals and Steps to Success<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Goals:<\/p>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, I will be able to:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Identify the importance of input in programs<\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Create and run programs that include user interaction<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Store and modify user inputs<\/span><\/li>\n<li>Utilize user input to make decisions in a program<\/li>\n<li>Allow user input to change program flow during flight<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Steps to Success:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Remote Control<\/li>\n<li>Takeoff<\/li>\n<li>While Loop<\/li>\n<li>Making Decisions<\/li>\n<li>Challenge: Super Program<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214803961e\" name=\"6a2214803961e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Remote control<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">User input is also great for remote controlling your drone! In this activity, you will program your keyboard to act as a remote, with \u201cwasd\u201d and \u201cijkl\u201d as the joysticks.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">First, import the CoDrone Mini and time libraries, and then create the drone object and pair your drone.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Next, include print commands for \u201c<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Command options<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">\u201d and then for each of the eight flight directions: positive and negative roll, pitch, yaw, and throttle. Make sure to include a \u201c<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">quit<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">\u201d option, which will land the drone.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Your menu should look something like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nprint(\"Command Options: \")\r\n\r\nprint(\"w: throttle up \\n\"\r\n\r\n    \"s: throttle down\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"a: yaw left\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"d: yaw right\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"i: pitch forward\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"k: pitch backward\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"j: roll left\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"l: roll right\\n\"\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\"q: quit\")<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214803968d\" name=\"6a2214803968d\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Takeoff<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Have your drone take off, and then set your trim if needed. This is for if your drone is drifting and you want to fix its pitch or roll, so if you\u2019re not having that issue, you can skip this step.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Next, create a variable called power and set its value to 40. This way, the default power will be 40%.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Your code should look like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">drone.takeoff()\r\n\r\ndrone.set_trim(0,0) # Adjust for your drone\r\n\r\npower = 40<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22148039705\" name=\"6a22148039705\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>While loop<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Set up a while loop so that while true, the program can take user input to make decisions. Next, reset the roll, pitch, yaw, and throttle to 0.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Create a variable called <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">direction<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\"> to store <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">input()<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">. In the parameters for <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">input()<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">, <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">write<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\"> &#8220;Input a command: &#8220;. <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">This will print the command, and when the user inputs a letter, it will be stored as an option and called later on.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Your code should look like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">while True:\r\n\r\n    drone.set_throttle(0)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_roll(0)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_yaw(0)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_pitch(0)\r\n\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 direction = input(\"Input a command: \")<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22148039794\" name=\"6a22148039794\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Making decisions<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Just like the user input menu, you need to use if, else if, and else statements to have your drone fly in different directions based on the user input. For the w key, use an if statement and then include a command for your drone to throttle up. In the parameters, use the power variable, which will have the drone move at 40% power.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">    if direction == \"w\":\r\n\r\n        drone.set_throttle(power)<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">For the other 7 flight commands, use else if statements. If you want the drone to go to the left, down, or backwards, put a negative sign next to the power variable in the parameters.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0elif direction == \"s\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    drone.set_throttle(-power)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0elif direction == \"a\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.set_yaw(-power)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0elif direction == \"d\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.set_yaw(power)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0elif direction == \"i\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.set_pitch(power)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0elif direction == \"k\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.set_pitch(-power)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0elif direction == \"j\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.set_roll(-power)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0elif direction == \"l\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.set_roll(power)\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>Write one more else if statement for the quit option. This will wait so the landing command isn\u2019t skipped, land the drone, and break out of the loop.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">    elif direction == \"q\":\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    time.sleep(0.05)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.land()\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0break<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Include an option that will let the user know they made a mistake if they input something that isn\u2019t an option in the program.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">    else:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    print(\"Not a command\")<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Finally, include a delay and a move command with one second in the parameters. This is what will actually move the drone, along with the code you wrote for each key!<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">    time.sleep(0.05)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move(1)<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Outside of the loop, include a print and drone.close() command so you know when you\u2019ve exited the program.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">print(\"Done\")\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221480397f7\" name=\"6a221480397f7\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge: Super Program<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">So you have a program that uses numbers to have your CoDrone Mini do different maneuvers, and you have another program that uses letters to have your CoDrone Mini fly in different directions. What if you combined them both in the same program?<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Rules:<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Must include an option to quit<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Must include a print command that lets the user know when they input an option that\u2019s not available<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 7 Goals and Steps to Success [vc_column_text]Goals: By the end of this lesson, I will be able to: Identify the importance of input in programs Create and run&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/2-7-user-input-controller\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4150,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3473,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1782,1848,1793,1826,1482,853,694,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2135,2125,2119,2121,2122,2134,2111,2136],"class_list":["post-3778","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-codrone-mini","lesson-tag-conditionals","lesson-tag-keyboard","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-remote-control","lesson-tag-user-input","module-level-2","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/3778","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":7,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/3778\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":4493,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/3778\/revisions\/4493"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"ht